# Lesson 4, batch 2: two finesses, one needed. ♠AQ in hand (finesse against East) and ♦AQ in dummy (finesse against West). The spade finesse loses; the diamond finesse wins.
title: Two chances
id: L04-29
type: exercise
lesson: 4
concepts: 4.1, 3.5
student: S
board: 111
dealer: S
vulnerable: NS

deal:            # spades  hearts  diamonds  clubs
  N  64     AQ83   AQ5    8742
  E  7532   62     JT82   T95
  S  AQ     KJ975  764    AK3
  W  KJT98  T4     K93    QJ6

auction:
  S 1H     = 13+ points, at least four hearts
     not *: 17 points and five hearts: open your long suit at the one level.
  W Pass
  N 3H     = 4+ hearts, an opening hand or better: game forcing
  E Pass
  S 4H     = Game
     not *: Partner's 3♥ is forcing to game. Bid it.
  W Pass
  N Pass
  E Pass

auction recap:
  You opened 1♥ and partner jumped to 3♥, showing four hearts and an opening
  hand or better, so you bid game. You are declarer, because you bid hearts
  first, and so West will lead to the first trick.

planning:
  trumps out: 4
  losers: 4
  spades: 1 = The queen, unless East has the king and you lead toward your ace-queen from dummy.
  hearts: 0 = Nine hearts with the ace, king, queen and jack.
  diamonds: 2 = Dummy's ace takes care of one. Dummy's queen takes care of another if West has the king and you lead toward the ace-queen.
  clubs: 1 = Your third club.
  remind: You can afford three losers in 4♥. Count the obvious losers suit by suit in your own hand: spades, hearts, diamonds, clubs.
  remind: You have two finesses, and you need only one of them to work. Which hand leads each one?
play:
  1   W CQ  N C2  E C5  S CA
      note: Four losers, one too many. Two finesses can each save a trick: the ♠Q if East has the king, and the ♦Q if West has the king. You need only one of them.
  2   S H5  W H4  N HA  E H2
      note: Draw trumps. Dummy's ace and queen first, the high cards from the short side. That leaves you in dummy, ready to lead a spade.
  3   N HQ  E H6  S H7  W HT
      note: Trumps are drawn, and you're in dummy.
  4   N S4  E S2  S SQ*  W SK
      note: You're in dummy, so start with the spade finesse: lead toward your ace-queen.
      SQ key: East played low, so finesse the queen. If East has the king, it wins.
      SQ not SA: Did you think about the finesse? East has played, so if East has the king, your queen wins. With the ace, the queen is sure to lose later.
  5   W CJ  N C4  E C9  S CK
      note: The spade finesse lost, but you have a second chance.
  6   S D4  W D3  N DQ*  E D2
      note: Lead a diamond toward dummy's ace-queen.
      DQ key: West played low, so finesse the queen. If West has the king, it wins.
      DQ not DA: Did you think about the second finesse? West has played, so if West has the king, the queen wins.
  7   N DA  E D8  S D6  W D9
      note: The queen won. Cash the ace.
  8   N D5  E DT  S D7  W DK
      note: Your third diamond is a loser.
  9   W SJ  N S6  E S3  S SA
  10  S C3  W C6  N C7  E CT
      note: And your third club.
  11  E S7  S H9  W S8  N C8
      note: The rest are yours.
  12  S HK  W ST  N H3  E S5
  13  S HJ  W S9  N H8  E DJ
      note: 10 tricks: 4♥ made.

play recap:
  The plan: four losers, one too many, and two finesses that could each
  save a trick. You needed only one of them to work.

  The spade finesse lost: West had the king. But you still had the diamond
  finesse, and it won, because West had that king too. With two finesses,
  you lose only when both fail, about one time in four. Each finesse leads
  toward the ace-queen, from whichever hand is opposite it.
